================================================================================
TEST T44986 -- deny/permit statements for standard access list
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09, box local time
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2, u3, u5; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWP-13990, objective "the deny and permit statements of a standard
          (1-99) access list behave correctly: permit, implicit deny, explicit
          deny, wildcard mask and first-match-wins", 5 behaviours, 7 steps as run
Build:    swi_c  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (netboots)
Configs:  swi_c.cfg, swi_b.cfg (running-config after setup; saved to startup: no).
          This case used swi_c only; swi_b carried no traffic.

VERDICT: PASS -- all five behaviours of a standard access list are demonstrated on this
         platform, each paired with its own control on the same two clients.
      standard list created ........... yes        (Standard IP access list 1, seq 4 permit)
      binds to an interface ........... NO         (access-group 1 -> % Access-list does not
                                                    exist; hardware ranges only)
      binds to the vty lines .......... yes        (vty access-class 1 in running-config)
      permit .......................... admits     (.65 ACCEPTED, SSH banner returned)
      implicit deny ................... blocks     (.66 TIMEOUT on that same list)
      explicit deny + permit any ...... inverse    (.65 TIMEOUT, .66 ACCEPTED)
      wildcard mask 0.0.0.1 ........... selects    (.65 ACCEPTED inside, .66 TIMEOUT outside)
      first match wins ................ yes        (permit at seq 4 beats deny at seq 8)
      removal ......................... clean      (no vty access-class restored both clients)
CAVEAT:  The case's own expectation that a standard list attaches to an interface does not
         hold on this platform, so the five behaviours were exercised at the binding point
         that does exist -- `vty access-class`, against SSH to the DUT's own vty. That is
         CONTROL-PLANE filtering: this run did NOT establish how a standard list behaves on
         transit traffic, because the platform offers no way to bind one there. Only two
         source addresses and only tcp/22 were used; the extended numbered range (1300-1999)
         that `vty access-class` also accepts was not exercised, and nothing was reloaded, so
         persistence of these lists across a restart was not measured.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
