            cannot choose which of the two addresses it comes from; console driving with
            tools/ckcon.py and tools/cfg.py on tb277

STEP 0 -- baseline: where a standard list binds here, and both clients reach
          the vty with nothing applied                                     done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A standard (1-99) list CANNOT be bound to an interface on this platform. Measured:
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 1 permit 10.38.22.65 0.0.0.0
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group 1
  % Access-list does not exist
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 1
  Standard IP access list 1
      4 permit 10.38.22.65                  <-- the list plainly DOES exist; `access-group`
                                                takes only 3000-3699, 4000-4699 or a named
                                                hardware list, and reports a standard list as
                                                non-existent rather than as the wrong type

  The Cisco-style line-mode form is not available either:
x960-56YSQ(config-line)#access-class 1
                             ^
  % Invalid input detected at '^' marker.

  The binding point that DOES exist is global, not under `line vty`:
x960-56YSQ(config)#vty access-class 1
x960-56YSQ#show running-config | include vty
  line vty 0 7
  vty access-class 1                        <-- accepted and present in the running config

  The four lists the behaviours are measured with:
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 1
  Standard IP access list 1
      4 permit 10.38.22.65
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 2
  Standard IP access list 2
      4 deny   10.38.22.65
      8 permit any
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 3
  Standard IP access list 3
      4 permit 10.38.22.64 0.0.0.1          <-- wildcard: covers .64 and .65 ONLY
x960-56YSQ#show access-list 4
  Standard IP access list 4
      4 permit 10.38.22.65
      8 deny   10.38.22.65                  <-- same address, opposite actions, permit first

  Baseline with no access-class applied:
  10.38.22.65    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'
  10.38.22.66    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'
                                            <-- both clients reach the vty before any list is
                                                applied, so a later refusal is attributable to
                                                the access-class and not to reachability

STEP 1 -- "permit statements work"                                         PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#vty access-class 1        (permit 10.38.22.65)
  10.38.22.65    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'
  => STEP 1 PASS: the address the single permit entry names is let through to the vty.

STEP 2 -- "the implicit deny works"                                        PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Same list 1 still applied; no entry covers 10.38.22.66.
  10.38.22.66    -> TIMEOUT (blocked)
  => STEP 2 PASS: an address no rule covers is blocked, so a standard (software) ACL
     implicitly DENIES what it does not permit. The pair .65 ACCEPTED / .66 TIMEOUT under one
     unchanged list is the proof: the only variable is the client address.

STEP 3 -- "deny statements work"                                           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#vty access-class 2        (deny 10.38.22.65, then permit any)
  10.38.22.65    -> TIMEOUT (blocked)
  10.38.22.66    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'
  => STEP 3 PASS: the explicitly denied address is blocked while the trailing `permit any`
     admits the other client. This is the exact inverse of STEP 1 / STEP 2 on the same two
     clients, so each result is the other's control: the outcome follows the rules, not the
     client.

STEP 4 -- "wildcard masks select the right addresses"                      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#vty access-class 3        (permit 10.38.22.64 0.0.0.1)
  The mask 0.0.0.1 leaves only the last bit free, so the entry covers 10.38.22.64 and
  10.38.22.65 and nothing else.
  10.38.22.65    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'   (inside the pair)
  10.38.22.66    -> TIMEOUT (blocked)                              (immediately outside it)
  => STEP 4 PASS: the wildcard admits an address inside the range it describes and blocks one
     immediately outside it. A mask that was ignored, or treated as a host match, would not
     have produced this split.

STEP 5 -- "rules are evaluated top to bottom, first match wins"            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#vty access-class 4        (permit 10.38.22.65 THEN deny 10.38.22.65)
  Both entries name the SAME address with opposite actions; only their order distinguishes
  them, so the result can only be explained by evaluation order.
  10.38.22.65    -> ACCEPTED  banner=b'SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_10.5\r\n'
  => STEP 5 PASS: the permit at sequence 4 wins over the deny at sequence 8. Evaluation stops
     at the first match and does not fall through to the later contradicting entry.

  The five behaviours together:

    step  behaviour            list  .65                  .66                  verdict
    1     permit               1     ACCEPTED             -                    PASS
    2     implicit deny        1     -                    TIMEOUT (blocked)    PASS
    3     deny                 2     TIMEOUT (blocked)    ACCEPTED             PASS
    4     wildcard mask        3     ACCEPTED             TIMEOUT (blocked)    PASS
    5     first match wins     4     ACCEPTED             -                    PASS

STEP 6 -- removal                                                          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#no vty access-class <n>
  Issued between every measurement above, and each removal was followed by a re-test that
  showed both clients reaching the vty again -- the STEP 0 state. (Recorded as a result at
  the time rather than captured per-client, so no client output is quoted for this step.)
  => STEP 6 PASS: removing the access-class restores both clients, which is what makes each
     row of the table above attributable to its own list rather than to a leftover binding.
